The `visitGeoShapeWrap` test method is incorrectly using `false` for the `wrapLongitude` parameter instead of `true`. This will not test the wrapping functionality as intended.
```java
var bbox = SpatialEnvelopeVisitor.visit(geometry, false);
```
Should be:
```java
var bbox = SpatialEnvelopeVisitor.visit(geometry, true);
```
The `assertCrossesDateline` method in the test class should check for the validity of the visitor before asserting the result. This ensures that the visitor has processed valid geometries.
```java
private void assertCrossesDateline(SpatialEnvelopeVisitor visitor, boolean crossesDateline) {
    assertTrue(visitor.isValid());
    var result = visitor.getResult();
    if (crossesDateline) {
        assertThat("Crosses dateline, minx>maxx", result.getMinX(), greaterThanOrEqualTo(result.getMaxX()));
    } else {
        assertThat("Does not cross dateline, minx<maxx", result.getMinX(), lessThanOrEqualTo(result.getMaxX()));
    }
}
```
The `visit` method for `Circle` should be implemented or clearly documented as unsupported. Currently, it throws an exception without a clear explanation of why circles are not supported.
```java
@Override
public Boolean visit(Circle circle) throws RuntimeException {
    // TODO: Support circle, if given CRS (needed for radius to x/y coordinate transformation)
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Circle is not supported");
}
```
Consider adding a comment explaining the reason or implementing the functionality if possible.
The `isValid` method in `GeoPointVisitor` should handle cases where only one of `minNegX` or `maxPosX` (or vice versa) has been updated, which could lead to incorrect results.
```java
@Override
